<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Sarbadars</h1><p class="st-meta">1337 CE – 1386 CE (approx.) · capital Sabzevar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A rebel regime in western Khurasan, born of a revolt against Mongol tax collectors, that ruled through a mix of military leaders and Shia dervishes.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the 1330s, as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> disintegrated, villagers at Bashtin near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabzevar">Sabzevar</a> rose against abusive Mongol officials. Their leaders took the name sarbadar, 'head on the gallows', meaning they would rather die than submit. Under Abd al-Razzaq and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wajih-al-din-masud">Wajih al-Din Mas'ud</a> they took Sabzevar and built a state in western Khurasan.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Sarbadars had no hereditary dynasty: power passed among military chiefs, often violently, and alongside them stood the Shaykhiyya, a Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> movement founded by Shaykh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalifa">Khalifa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-juri">Shaykh Hasan Juri</a>, whose dervishes expected the coming of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>. The state minted coins with Shia formulas and defeated the Mongol claimant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togha-temur">Togha Temür</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> described their egalitarian, puritanical regime.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last leader, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-yi-muayyad">Ali-yi Mu'ayyad</a>, submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in 1381 and died in his service in 1386, after which the Sarbadar lands joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>. Historians see them as an early Shia state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sarbadars.jpg" alt="Sarbadars" width="320" height="221"><figcaption>Image: DesmembramientoDelIljanato1337–1432-pt.svg: Renato de carvalho ferreira derivative work: Rowanwindwhistler (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Post-Ilkhanid_territories_(1345).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1337 CE – 1386 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sabzevar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abd al-Razzaq, Wajih al-Din Mas'ud, Ali-yi Mu'ayyad</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Twelver Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sarbadar republic, Sarbadarids</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1362&amp;focus=sarbadars" aria-label="Sarbadars on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6106 861 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sarbadars lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6426 1117L6134 1224M6426 1117L6582 1190"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0M6582 1190h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6426 1117h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6426 1117h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1362&amp;focus=sarbadars">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
